WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.140 --> 00:00:06.060
What we want to do is build this map of
the solar system. So what does that mean

00:00:06.060 --> 00:00:12.750
in a modern day and age? Well, first off, in order to know where the

00:00:12.750 --> 00:00:17.250
asteroids are now, what I need to do is
I gotta build telescopes. I need to

00:00:17.250 --> 00:00:21.029
observe an asteroid and I need to watch
it move for some period of time so I can

00:00:21.029 --> 00:00:24.000
figure out what its orbit is. And then I
need to have math, and I need to

00:00:24.000 --> 00:00:30.840
understand orbital mechanics and I can
calculate the the trajectory of that one

00:00:30.840 --> 00:00:33.690
particular asteroid. And now I
need to repeat by a million times.

00:00:33.690 --> 00:00:36.840
There's a million or so asteroids.
And I need to

00:00:36.840 --> 00:00:41.670
update these things. So the first thing
you need to build a really good map

00:00:41.670 --> 00:00:45.690
at the solar system is telescopes.
Now this isn't that much different than

00:00:45.690 --> 00:00:48.989
how we map the surface of the earth.
we had spy satellites which

00:00:48.989 --> 00:00:52.379
became commercial satellites looking
down at the earth. But the original

00:00:52.379 --> 00:00:57.320
ones were built by governments. And that
data was held secret and tightly held,

00:00:57.320 --> 00:01:03.449
eventually became open and eventually
became publicly available. And a couple

00:01:03.449 --> 00:01:06.900
of companies were able to take these
publicly available information and make

00:01:06.900 --> 00:01:12.270
really good use of it different than the
way the CIA analyst and those types of

00:01:12.270 --> 00:01:17.100
folks looked at things. They built things
that were usable. They built Google Earth.

00:01:17.100 --> 00:01:22.830
They built Google Maps. They built MapQuest. What we
want to do is follow that same tack. We

00:01:22.830 --> 00:01:26.700
want to take data from these telescopes - 
and these are some interesting

00:01:26.700 --> 00:01:30.979
telescopes - the one up there is one
called Pan-STARRS. It's in Hawaii, and

00:01:30.979 --> 00:01:36.990
it's on the top of Maui. And it is the
current most powerful telescope for

00:01:36.990 --> 00:01:41.939
finding and tracking asteroids on Earth. The
one in the middle is one called the

00:01:41.939 --> 00:01:47.850
Large Synoptic Survey Telescope. It is going to be the world's largest

00:01:47.850 --> 00:01:53.250
survey telescope for finding asteroids
and looking into the formation and

00:01:53.250 --> 00:01:58.799
evolution of the universe. That
is being built currently in Chile by the

00:01:58.799 --> 00:02:04.350
National Science Foundation for about
700 million dollars and it becomes

00:02:04.350 --> 00:02:11.330
operational in
two and a half years. And so that

00:02:11.330 --> 00:02:17.600
thing there is about 50 times more
powerful than this one. And so what's

00:02:17.600 --> 00:02:20.990
going to happen here is that the
publicly available datasets - remember

00:02:20.990 --> 00:02:24.620
this is paid for by the US government - 
is going to explode.

00:02:24.620 --> 00:02:27.440
There's going to be an enormous number
of new discoveries. Now, it's not going to

00:02:27.440 --> 00:02:30.230
find all the asteroids we want to find,
but it's going to do a heck of a lot

00:02:30.230 --> 00:02:33.709
better than anything out there right now.
So what it means is we're gonna have a lot

00:02:33.709 --> 00:02:38.209
of data. What are we going to do with it?
How are we preparing to deal with this

00:02:38.209 --> 00:02:41.630
data? How do we make that map out of this
data? Because really what this thing is

00:02:41.630 --> 00:02:46.220
finding is not...it's not producing a map
anymore than Google Maps is the same as

00:02:46.220 --> 00:02:49.550
a bunch of photographs of the earth
taken by spy satellites. Somebody had to

00:02:49.550 --> 00:02:53.540
build a system that allows you to
navigate around and allows you to do

00:02:53.540 --> 00:02:57.350
things like turn-by-turn directions,
you know, place my store here, that sort

00:02:57.350 --> 00:03:01.640
of stuff. All those things are what made
Google Maps useful that's not been done

00:03:01.640 --> 00:03:05.720
for the solar system for navigating to
asteroids, for calculating if an

00:03:05.720 --> 00:03:10.700
asteroid might hit the earth, for
laying resource claims, for doing

00:03:10.700 --> 00:03:14.530
deep-space navigation. All those things
are going to be services built on a map.

00:03:14.530 --> 00:03:19.340
So maps are changing from
two-dimensional to four-dimensional and

00:03:19.340 --> 00:03:24.080
they're changing from static objects to
services. And if you think about what

00:03:24.080 --> 00:03:26.239
this map is going to be, it's going to be
a service. It's going to be something

00:03:26.239 --> 00:03:32.890
that exists in computers that people can
access and use and build off of.

